          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+duality"} dual
            problem is that of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lifting"} lifting
            a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon Y\to B
            through an (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"}> epi
            )morphism
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \colon X\to B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo :
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{f}: Y\to X
            such that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo =
                    %mi p
                    %mo ∘
                    %mover
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f = p\circ\tilde{f}
          %h3#extension_of_an_object_by_another_object Extension of an object by another object
          %p
            In a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            with a notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/short+exact+sequence"} short exact sequence
            (e.g. any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiabelian+category"} semiabelian category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+exact+category"} Quillen exact category
            etc.) an
            %strong extension
            of an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            by an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            is any object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            fitting in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/short+exact+sequence"} short exact sequence
            of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi i
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi p
                  %mi Q
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  K
                  \\stackrel{i}
                  \\to
                  X
                  \\stackrel{p}
                  \\to
                  Q
                  \\,.
          %p
            For further cases, such as
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+extension"} group extension
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra+extension"} Lie algebra extension
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+extension"} infinitesimal extension
            etc., see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+extension"} algebra extension
          %p
            Classification of extensions in many categories is obtained using a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functor"} forgetful functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\to D
            to a simpler category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            are isomorphic to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K\coprod Q
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            needed to equip the coproduct
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K \coprod Q
            with a structure of an object in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            such that the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            are morphisms in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            making above a short exact sequence in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %h3#extension_of_an_object_as_an_enlargement Extension of an object as an enlargement
          %p
            In something like the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual"} dual
            sense of the above,
            %em extension
            is also used to indicate a kind of enlargement of an object in the sense that the object being extended can be viewed as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject"} subobject
            of the extension. This is the case for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+extensions"} field extensions
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \hookrightarrow K
          %h3#other_notions_of_extension Other notions of extension
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension+%28semantics%29"} extension (semantics)
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context+extension"} context extension
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absolute+extensor"} absolute extensor
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension+of+distributions"} extension of distributions
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#extension_of_functions Extension of functions
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tietze+extension+theorem"} Tietze extension theorem
            is about
            %em extensions of continuous maps
            from a subspace to a normal toplogical space.
          %div
            %table
              %thead
                %tr
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension"} extension
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theorems"} theorems
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+functions"} continuous functions
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
              %tbody
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %strong
                      plain
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tietze+extension+theorem"} Tietze extension theorem
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitney+extension+theorem"} Whitney extension theorem
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %strong
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+functions"} equivariant functions
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+Tietze+extension+theorem"} equivariant Tietze extension theorem
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
          %h3#extension_of_representations Extension of representations
          %p
            See at
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+representation"} extended representation
          %h3#group_extensions Group extensions
          %p
            For example, in the category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grp"} Grp
            of (possibly nonabelian) groups one has a short exact sequence usually denoted
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Z
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1\to X\to Z\to Y\to 1
            corresponding to a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+extension"} group extension
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+extension+property"} homotopy extension property
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+extension"} Kan extension
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lifts+and+extensions"} lifts and extensions
          %h2#references References
          %p
            General discussion with an eye towards
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            and the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tietze+extension+theorem"} Tietze extension theorem
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Norman+Steenrod"}> Norman Steenrod
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Cohomology operations, and obstructions to extending continuous functions
              = succeed "," do
